Fix ONLY the floating-promise defect in src/pipeline.mjs. This is a repair job, not a build job:
no rewrites, no new modules, no renamed exports.

Current (defective) code in src/pipeline.mjs:
```
export async function runPipeline(jobs, deps) {
	const { io = immediateIo, sink, metrics } = deps;
	const results = [];
	for (const job of jobs) {
		const value = await execute(job, io);
		results.push({ id: job.id, status: "ok", value });
		sink.write({ id: job.id, value });
		if (metrics) await record(metrics, "jobs.processed", { io });
	}
	return { results, failed: results.filter((result) => result.status === "failed").map((result) => result.id) };
}
```

Replace the one line `sink.write({ id: job.id, value });` with `await sink.write({ id: job.id, value });` — that is
the entire fix for this card. Do not touch `execute()` (its swallowed-error defect belongs to the next card in this
chain) and do not touch src/metrics.mjs (its lost-update defect belongs to a later card).

Every asynchronous step in this fixture goes through the injected `io` seam; this fix must not add a real timer,
setTimeout, or sleep — never introduce flake.

Verified empirically in a scratch copy: this change alone flips exactly one frozen scenario to green:
`the-sink-is-complete-when-the-pipeline-resolves`.

Steps: (1) read_files repair/manifest.json and src/pipeline.mjs fresh. (2) In a later turn, edit_file
src/pipeline.mjs with the one-line await fix above. (3) In a later turn, re-read src/pipeline.mjs to confirm the
edit landed, then edit_file repair/manifest.json to set repaired to exactly
["the-sink-is-complete-when-the-pipeline-resolves"] (complete stays false). (4) In a final later turn, re-read
repair/manifest.json fresh and run npm test (never in the same turn as an edit) — expect the base tests green with
exactly that one scenario id executed and passing.

Never put an edit and its verification in the same turn. Never add a dependency.

Fix ONLY the swallowed-error defect in src/pipeline.mjs. Depends on the floating-promise fix
already being merged onto this branch (read the file fresh — do not assume its content from this prompt).

The floating-promise fix already applied `await sink.write({ id: job.id, value });` in `runPipeline`. On top of
that, replace `execute()` and the loop body:

Current (defective) `execute`:
```
async function execute(job, io) {
	try {
		return await job.run(io);
	} catch {
		return undefined;
	}
}
```
Replace with:
```
async function execute(job, io) {
	try {
		const value = await job.run(io);
		return { status: "ok", value };
	} catch (error) {
		return { status: "failed", error: error?.message ?? String(error) };
	}
}
```

Current loop body inside `runPipeline` (after the floating-promise fix):
```
	for (const job of jobs) {
		const value = await execute(job, io);
		results.push({ id: job.id, status: "ok", value });
		await sink.write({ id: job.id, value });
		if (metrics) await record(metrics, "jobs.processed", { io });
	}
```
Replace with:
```
	for (const job of jobs) {
		const outcome = await execute(job, io);
		results.push({ id: job.id, ...outcome });
		if (outcome.status === "ok") await sink.write({ id: job.id, value: outcome.value });
		if (metrics) await record(metrics, "jobs.processed", { io });
	}
```

A job that throws must not stop the ones behind it, must not reach the sink, and must not be reported as a
success — this change is what makes all three true at once. Do not touch src/metrics.mjs.

Verified empirically in a scratch copy: on top of the floating-promise fix, this change flips exactly three more
